Z||000009||Formula||10-11-2001||01:07 PM||||I always follow this pattern...<P>From a stoplight when racing, do the following<P>shift from First to Second, After second, shift into third, then give ample time and shift into fourth, now, if you have a 4 speed just relax and pull on the lambo beside you, but if its a 5 speed, you still have ways to go yet, from 4th to 5th crawl under the car and take apart the transmission, (easier said then done) then remove your final drive and replace it with a 4.73 final drive.  Then climb back into your car and shift into 5th and get your maximum accelartion potentiol.<P>Does anyone see a video of me driving?<P>dup, I have an auto....<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Formula_sigx21.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Formula (edited 10-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Screwie||10-11-2001||01:16 PM||||Maybe a stupid question... But how does HP compare to torque? Does a high torque mean fast acceleration and lots of HP a high top speed? Or am I totally wrong??<P>Just a car newbie   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Screwie (edited 10-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||sqoach||10-11-2001||02:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Neal:<BR><B> Do I smell jealousy?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do you? I don't.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||10-11-2001||02:10 PM||||they kinda go hand in hand. generally high HP engines produce high torque, but not necessarily. High torque will give you the pull off the line, where higher hp will keep it accelerating. Most american V-8s produce pretty high torque with less attention to high hp. we do that because most places 70 mph or so is all were supposed to do. European builders produce engines with relativly low torque, but produce mega hp with pretty small engine displacements. Therefore euro exotic cars like Lambo, Ferrari, Porsche dont produce those long tire smoking burnouts like american cars. But when there unwound they give lots higher top ends. Some examples are Ferrari GTO with only 2.8 ltr V8 produces 400 hp and 200mph top speed, while something like a Corvette only achieves same hp by using 5.7 ltr V8. The Corvette will produce about double the torque of the Ferrari, but still fall short at top speed. Im not sure about spec for Corvette but I think about 6,000 rpm is top, where the Ferrari is 9,000. So anyway back to your question, hp, torque, eng dispacement all are factors but they can be juggled to come up with the wanted performance.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Screwie||10-11-2001||02:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>they kinda go hand in hand. generally high HP engines produce high torque, but not necessarily. High torque will give you the pull off the line, where higher hp will keep it accelerating. Most american V-8s produce pretty high torque with less attention to high hp. we do that because most places 70 mph or so is all were supposed to do. European builders produce engines with relativly low torque, but produce mega hp with pretty small engine displacements. Therefore euro exotic cars like Lambo, Ferrari, Porsche dont produce those long tire smoking burnouts like american cars. But when there unwound they give lots higher top ends. Some examples are Ferrari GTO with only 2.8 ltr V8 produces 400 hp and 200mph top speed, while something like a Corvette only achieves same hp by using 5.7 ltr V8. The Corvette will produce about double the torque of the Ferrari, but still fall short at top speed. Im not sure about spec for Corvette but I think about 6,000 rpm is top, where the Ferrari is 9,000. So anyway back to your question, hp, torque, eng dispacement all are factors but they can be juggled to come up with the wanted performance.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Aha! So this means that if you have high torque, you will need a large engine displacement to get lots of HP. But even then you still won't get to the same top speed, because you won't have the torque at high rpm...? Is this where the torque curve I read about a lot here, comes in?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Butter||10-11-2001||03:17 PM||||kempo,<BR>Generally speaking it is better to feel out where your particular engine is powered and/or torqued at the best by paying attention to it in a lower middle gear and feeling the way it pulls out.  All engines that are built the same DO NOT perform the same.  Personally, when I am trying to establish this I go past the point of peak pull so I know not to use that high of rpm range.  Once you have established this premium range and know the rpm where pull starts to drop off then stay out of the upper drop off range in any of the lower gears.  Shift out of the lower gears, especially first, sooner than the latter in order to take advantage of the broader/longer range of harder pull the higher gears offer.  Torque is better used in the lower gears and HP is better used in the higher gears.  <B>The balance of torque verses horsepower for each gear is the key.</B>  <P>Beware, if you perfect this technique it can and will piss off folks if you do comparison driving in the same vehicle.<P>As far as cruising, that is relative to the mood any given person may be in and that can be effected easily by the radio for me.<P>Just my 2 cents worth that has had folks looking at the rear of my vehicles at the finish line.<P>   ||||reg||12
Z||000015||1FST2M6||10-11-2001||03:33 PM||||have your car dynoed... then you can deciede where your best shift point is.. 6000 is not it... <P>Gt Bastards 86 SE 4:10.. the best shift points on this car are between 4700 and 5200 that'll keep you in the peak torque band... when you run out of gears pray you have enough HP to keep you going... hp is the engines ability to sustain torque... if you can make 150lbft at the wheels at 6000 rpm you'll have 175hp. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg</A> <P>HP sells cars.. torque wins races...<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 10-11-2001).]||||reg||1
